## Summary
A personalised daily news briefing delivered to my inbox every morning, generated by a multi-agent system built on Google ADK. Three specialist agents — a fetcher, an analyst, and a writer — each handle one part of the pipeline. The orchestrator sequences them, passes context between them, and produces a clean HTML email by 7am.

## Problem
News aggregators surface volume, not relevance. I wanted a briefing that matched my actual interests — AI, cloud infrastructure, industrial tech, business strategy — filtered, summarised, and written in a format I'd actually read over coffee.

## Solution
A multi-agent pipeline running on Cloud Run, triggered daily by Cloud Scheduler. The fetcher agent pulls from NewsAPI and a set of curated RSS feeds. The analyst agent summarises each article with Gemini and scores relevance against a personal interest profile. The writer agent assembles the top stories into a structured HTML briefing with section headers, one-paragraph summaries, and source links.

## Architecture
Three-layer pipeline: Cloud Scheduler fires daily at 7am → Root Orchestrator (Google ADK SequentialAgent) → Fetcher Agent (NewsAPI + RSS tools) → Analyst Agent (Gemini summarise + relevance score) → Writer Agent (HTML renderer + SendGrid) → Firestore article store → inbox.

## Key Features
- Google ADK SequentialAgent orchestrates three sub-agents with shared session state
- Fetcher pulls from 12 sources across AI, cloud, finance, and industrial tech
- Analyst scores each article 1-10 for relevance using a Gemini prompt with personal interest profile
- Writer produces clean HTML email — no filler, no ads, no algorithmic noise
- Firestore deduplication — articles seen in the past 7 days are skipped automatically
- Runs at 7am daily with no manual intervention

## Technical Details
- **Orchestration:** Google ADK SequentialAgent — fetcher → analyst → writer, session state passed between agents
- **AI layer:** Gemini 1.5 Flash on Vertex AI for summarisation and relevance scoring
- **Storage:** Firestore for article deduplication and read history
- **Delivery:** SendGrid API called by the writer agent's email tool
- **Infrastructure:** Cloud Run service triggered by Cloud Scheduler HTTP call
- **Language:** Python throughout

## What I learned
This was the foundation for understanding multi-agent orchestration in practice. The biggest lesson was designing the session state schema carefully — the fetcher writes raw articles, the analyst annotates them with scores and summaries, and the writer reads the annotated list. Getting that handoff clean is what separates a working pipeline from a fragile one. This pattern now informs how I design enterprise multi-agent POCs at DataPiper.

